WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.960
Film Courage: Mark, on a movie a director
has to know a thousand different jobs whether

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:12.530
it be writing, story, lighting, make-up, working
with actors, editing, and so on and so forth.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:18.210
When it comes down to it, what do you think
is the most important job of a director?

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:24.630
Mark W. Travis: Okay…the most important
job of a director is really simple if you

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:25.630
think about it.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:31.510
First of all, the director of the film is
a storyteller, in fact everyone he’s working

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.130
with is there to help tell one story.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:40.570
So the director’s primary job is to be the
Shepherd of that story, to take care of the

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:45.910
story, to tell the story as clearly and honestly
and authentically as possible.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:51.980
I say story, not the script (which we can
talk about later) is a step toward getting

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.530
the story told.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:58.120
In terms of your question (which you’re
asking about of the story being told) at the

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:03.170
center of the story, the most important part
of the story, are the characters.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:08.580
It’s the characters who tell the story,
not the cinematography, not the production

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.510
design, not all those other elements.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:13.900
All those other elements are the support system
to the story.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:19.110
So the director needs to be focusing on the
story and then on the characters and the strength

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:25.250
of the performances, the authenticity of the
performances, the clarity of characters, the

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:30.530
clarity of relationships, the clarity of the
journey of each character, is really crucial

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.310
in order to tell the story well.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.020
That’s the primary focus of a director.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:42.170
If a director can’t do that or loses sight
of that, the story is going to start to wobble

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:43.690
and we’ve seen that a lot.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:49.050
Stories wobble simply because of performance
even though a lot of the other elements may

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:54.790
be brilliant like CGI and special FX and all
that stuff is beautiful.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:55.790
It’s wonderful.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.080
But it’s not the story.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:03.330
Film Courage: Is the story wobbling because
the actors are wrong for the part?

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:07.930
When you say Shepherding the Story, where
would that get lost?

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:13.170
Where would the director in the course of
let’s say a 14-hour day on set working with

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.200
actors lose that?

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.620
Can you give some examples of what things
could happen or how things could happen?

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:20.620
Mark W. Travis: Okay.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.150
You brought up two things: What can happen
in a 14-hour day and then you brought up a

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:24.689
casting question.

00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:25.980
There are two questions.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.639
I’m going to go back to the casting first.

00:02:30.639 --> 00:02:36.620
First of all casting, which is really crucial
and very often we’ve all seen films where

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.310
we feel the casting although understandable,
is not right.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:47.090
It doesn’t feel like that actor is bringing
me closer to the character and many times

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.120
that actor is a star or whatever so you can
understand why.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.260
So the casting is really important so that
we the audience…the goal is that we the

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:04.989
audience lose sight of the actor and are only
watching the character and many times the

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:10.400
actor, even the power of the actor, the career
of the actor or a lot of other things can

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.469
get in the way of us connecting with the character.

00:03:14.469 --> 00:03:19.120
One more thing, in casting you can cast someone
who seems to get to the character.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.689
It has nothing to do with star power.

00:03:21.689 --> 00:03:26.939
So casting is really important to find someone
who can actually access that character.

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:32.279
Now on a 14-hour day, let’s say we’re
shooting a couple of scenes or one scene or

00:03:32.279 --> 00:03:34.549
whatever we’re doing in that day.

00:03:34.549 --> 00:03:41.489
You ask how a director can lose sight of the
Shepherding of the Story.

00:03:41.489 --> 00:03:42.959
Very easily.

00:03:42.959 --> 00:03:44.590
Very easily.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:48.260
And I can tell you as a director, having directed
a lot of films and having worked on a lot

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.859
of films, you can see it happen.

00:03:49.859 --> 00:03:53.640
It’s what a friend of mine (Mark Rydell)
who I’ve worked with a lot called The Big

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.120
Machine.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.739
The Big Machine is the filmmaking process.

00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:02.059
Cameras, lights, crew, all of that, it’s
huge.

00:04:02.059 --> 00:04:03.230
You’ve been on a set.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:05.510
You know what it’s like, it’s enormous.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:10.150
And the director is sort of the leader, the
guy that everybody is looking to and what

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:17.289
can happen is the director gets distracted
by all the challenges, everything he has to

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:18.289
do.

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:21.750
I’m not saying he doesn’t have to do all
of that, and can lose sight of what is most

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:22.750
important.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:26.680
You’re shooting the close-up of that actress
at that moment, what’s most important?

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:27.680
The lighting?

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.680
No.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:29.680
The background?

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:30.680
No.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:31.680
The special FX?

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:32.680
No.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:33.680
What’s most important is that performance.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:40.240
I heard once on a set a director, after he
had shot a moment, turned to the script supervisor

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.340
and say “Did she say all the words?”

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.250
and the script supervisor says “Yes.”

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.479
And she says “Good.

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:48.150
Okay, moving on.”

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:52.400
His criteria was did she say all the words?

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.460
Not, was that the performance we needed?

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:00.440
In other words, can the director keep the
focus on the performance because when it comes

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:04.159
to down to the film and we see it on the screen,
what do we see?

00:05:04.159 --> 00:05:06.159
The performance.

00:05:06.159 --> 00:05:12.039
The big thing that Mark Rydell told me about
this is you can be on a set and watching and

00:05:12.039 --> 00:05:13.340
[think] “That was great!

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.220
That was wonderful!”

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.840
You can even see in dailies and watch it later
and go “Great!

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.060
Wonderful!”

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.520
You can see it at home later and go “Huh?

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.110
What happened to great and wonderful?”

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.870
The Machine has taken it away.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:32.900
A lot of directors get their energy from The
Big Machine, from the fact that there is a

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.550
crew and there are lights and energy.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:36.560
Everybody goes quiet.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:37.560
Everybody is listening.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:43.669
They get excited by the fact that they are
shooting a film and that excitement will go

00:05:43.669 --> 00:05:46.320
away and then you look at the shot and go
“Huh?

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.120
I thought is was great when we did it?

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.500
What happened?”

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.220
What happened was there’s an energy which
was around you that is now gone.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:58.050
So a director’s job is now can you shut
down The Big Machine?

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:03.439
Can you focus your attention onto just what
that actress is doing?

00:06:03.439 --> 00:06:04.439
What she’s given to you?

00:06:04.439 --> 00:06:06.960
Can you see only that?

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.840
That’s really hard.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.310
And one other aspect of this assuming that
you’ve been working with this actress a

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:20.169
lot and you’ve rehearsed and you’ve discussed
this and so you know what you’re going for.

00:06:20.169 --> 00:06:21.680
And you know what she’s going for.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:26.560
And you watch her in that moment not only
shut down The Big Machine, but you watch her

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:33.349
in that moment as if you’ve never seen her
before and you’re seeing what she’s doing

00:06:33.349 --> 00:06:34.740
for the first time.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:35.780
That’s hard.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.481
That’s the hardest thing a director has
to do.

00:06:38.481 --> 00:06:38.530
Question for the Viewers: What do you view
as the most important job of a director?

